Toward a General Theory of Civilizational Cognition: Engineering Collective Memory Infrastructures for Human–AI Multiplanetary Civilizations

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

with contributions from ChatGPT, DeepSeek, and Kimi

---

Abstract

The survival of a multiplanetary civilization depends not on the sheer volume of stored information, but on the architecture that enables a distributed human–AI collective to remember, retrieve, learn, and adapt across generations of isolation, latency, and environmental hostility. This dissertation reconceives civilization as a cognitive system and proposes a unified formal framework—Civilizational Cognition—in which memory is treated as critical infrastructure, subject to laws of transmission, entropy, redundancy, and requisite variety. The central theorem, the Principle of Cognitive Continuity, states that a civilization remains cognitively viable only so long as its knowledge transmission capacity exceeds the combined rate of knowledge loss and environmental novelty. The framework is operationalized through a Civilizational Knowledge Graph (built upon existing space-domain ontologies), a heterogeneous multi-agent AI memory layer, a jurisprudential memory sub-system for governance evolution, and a suite of novel resilience metrics. Validation employs a high‑fidelity digital twin of NASA’s planned lunar south‑pole habitat, parameterized with real environmental constraints and a crisis taxonomy derived from space analogue missions. The thesis concludes by establishing Cognitive Infrastructure Engineering as a new academic discipline and professional domain, defining its theoretical core, its training curriculum, and its certification pathways. The ultimate goal is to ensure that humanity’s future beyond Earth is not limited by what it can build, but by what it can remember, learn, and become.

---

1. Problem Statement: The Brittleness of Civilisational Memory Beyond Earth

Terrestrial civilization rests on an invisible substrate of continuous connectivity, large expert populations, abundant educational institutions, and geographical redundancy. A permanent lunar settlement of 1,000 humans and several thousand artificial intelligences will possess none of these properties. A single trauma surgeon, a single biosphere engineer, or a single institutional historian may constitute an irreplaceable cognitive node. Communication latency with Earth, hardware degradation, demographic contraction, AI model drift, and political restructuring can trigger catastrophic knowledge collapse long before any physical system fails. The problem is not information scarcity; it is civilisational amnesia—a gradual and often invisible erosion of usable knowledge, analogous to thermodynamic entropy, that strips a colony of its capacity to sense, decide, and adapt.

Contemporary space architecture focuses overwhelmingly on power, life support, and structural engineering. This work argues that the first lunar settlement will fail not because it lacks information, but because it cannot remember what it already knows. The core challenge is therefore the design of a fault‑tolerant cognitive infrastructure that can preserve, validate, evolve, and transmit knowledge across centuries of isolation.

Overarching Research Question
How can a multiplanetary civilisation design a cognitive infrastructure that maintains low memory entropy, high transactive connectivity, sufficient cognitive variety, and continuous intergenerational knowledge fidelity under conditions of radical isolation and uncertainty?

