3. Operationalising the Framework: Architectural Artefacts and Metrics

3.1 The Civilizational Knowledge Graph (CKG)

The operational backbone is a Civilizational Knowledge Graph, a semantic network in which nodes (humans, AIs, institutions, habitats, laws, scientific discoveries, historical events) are connected by typed relations (created, depends_on, supersedes, contradicts, validates). The CKG does not start from scratch; it extends existing spacecraft equipment ontologies and anomaly event graphs already demonstrated in space mission analysis. A unified lunar colony ontology subsumes domain‑specific graphs for engineering, medicine, ecology, and governance. To mitigate the scarcity of human expertise, zero‑shot LLM‑driven ontology generation (achieving >80% hierarchical accuracy) is adopted for autonomous maintenance, with human‑expert verification loops ensuring critical precision.

3.2 Multi‑Agent AI Memory Architecture

Layer 3 is redesigned as a heterogeneous Multi‑Agent Cognitive Infrastructure with specialized roles:

· Archival Agents: maintain persistent knowledge stores with version control;
· Reasoning Agents: perform inference and consistency checking;
· Teaching Agents: adaptively transfer knowledge to new human members;
· Maintenance Agents: monitor model drift and initiate retraining.

Coordination uses Centralized Training with Decentralized Execution (CTDE) and graph‑based policy representations, achieving the high fault tolerance and autonomy validated in recent on‑orbit space AI surveys.

3.3 Tacit Knowledge Externalization Systems

To address the hardest problem—capturing knowledge that experts cannot fully articulate—the framework introduces Tacit Knowledge Externalization Systems:

1. AI‑mediated apprenticeship recording with multimodal sensor streams;
2. Augmented reality skill capture during maintenance tasks;
3. Decision‑trace logging with rationale extraction using explainable AI;
4. Periodic knowledge stress tests, where experts are temporarily withdrawn to verify system redundancy.

3.4 Jurisprudential Memory: Governance and Legal Evolution

Institutional Memory is expanded to include a Jurisprudential Memory sub‑layer that preserves the colony’s accumulated body of legal interpretations, dispute resolutions, and governance adaptations. It is designed to evolve semi‑independently of Earth law while maintaining compatibility with the Artemis Accords. This sub‑layer serves as the cognitive substrate for institutional evolution, resisting bureaucratic entropy through periodic constitutional stress tests and automated contradiction detection.

3.5 Quantitative Redundancy and Network Degradation Metrics

Moving beyond a heuristic “three copies” rule, the thesis defines formal redundancy metrics:

· Knowledge Node Criticality (KNC): structural and functional importance of a knowledge element in the CKG;
· Civilizational Baseline Subgraph (CBS): the minimum connected knowledge structure required for autonomous colony operation;
· Memory Redundancy Factor (MRF): ratio of actual to minimum required replication for each knowledge category.

3.6 Intergenerational Knowledge Fidelity Index (IKFI)

\text{IKFI} = w_a A_{\text{accuracy}} + w_c C_{\text{completeness}} + w_x X_{\text{accessibility}} + w_u U_{\text{cultural continuity}},

with mission‑specific weights w, enabling simulation of the maximum number of generational handoffs before critical knowledge degrades beyond recovery.

3.7 Cognitive Resilience Metrics (Operational)

From the theoretical concepts, four operational indicators are derived:

· Cognitive Recovery Time (CRT): time required to restore a lost critical capability;
· Knowledge Accessibility Score (KAS): probability of retrieving mission‑critical knowledge within a specified window;
· Expertise Redundancy Ratio (ERR): number of independent cognitive pathways to the same knowledge;
· Civilizational Memory Entropy S_C as a continuous monitoring index.

