7. Long‑Term Vision

The survival of a multiplanetary humanity depends on its ability to think as a species across time, distance, and cognitive substrate. This dissertation provides the first rigorous treatment of civilization as a computational entity whose viability is governed by quantifiable cognitive parameters. It does not merely describe how a colony might store information; it shows how a civilization can remember, learn, and evolve as a coherent, self‑aware intelligence. The ultimate ambition is not technological but civilisational: to ensure that wherever human beings travel, they carry not only their memories but the engineered capacity to remain a learning species. The Collective Memory Architect—and the discipline of Cognitive Infrastructure Engineering—will be as fundamental to the 22nd century as civil engineering was to the 20th. This work lays the foundation.

