Appendix D: Civilizational Cognitive Twin Simulation Design

D.1 System Dynamics Model

The simulation is a discrete‑time agent‑based model (time step = 1 day). The core differential equations from Appendix A are discretised and applied to each knowledge category.

· Population: 1000 humans with age structure; mortality, births, immigration/emigration.
· Human memory: Forgetting curve: retention = e^{-\lambda t} with \lambda depending on use frequency.
· AI memory: Models retrained weekly on new data; drift modelled as Gaussian noise added to embedding vectors.
· Institutional memory: Documents decay (become inaccessible) if not accessed for D_{\text{max}} days; new protocols created at rate proportional to novel incidents.

D.2 Crisis Scenarios and Injection Schedule

Scenario ID Origin Memory Layer Affected Propagation Injection Time (Year) Duration
SC‑01 Power grid failure (14‑day night) H, I, A Cascading to life support 7, 35, 78 14 days
SC‑02 Pandemic (unknown pathogen) H (only 2 physicians) Systemic 20 90 days
SC‑03 AI model drift (contradictory outputs) A Localized then systemic 45 ongoing
SC‑04 Political coup; institutional erasure I, C Systemic 60 180 days
SC‑05 Full communication blackout with Earth H, I, A, C Systemic 90 365 days

D.3 Metrics Computation

At each time step:

· CRT: measure time from injection of a critical knowledge loss event until that knowledge is re‑acquired or bypassed.
· KAS: for a set of 200 randomly sampled critical queries, percentage answered correctly within \theta.
· ERR: for each of the top‑100 KNC nodes, count independent paths in the CKG; ERR is the minimum across these.
· IKFI: computed at generational boundaries (every 25 simulated years).
· S_C: computed daily using current M_{\text{acc}} and M_{\text{total}}.

D.4 Implementation

Simulation built in Python using the Mesa framework. CKG stored in Neo4j with py2neo interface. Multi‑agent communication via ZeroMQ. Results logged to TimescaleDB for time‑series analysis.

